109326.y_answer:
\textbf{Evolutionary mechanisms of antibiotic resistance:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Selection pressure} from widespread/sub-therapeutic antibiotic use kills susceptible cells, favouring resistant variants.
\item \textbf{Genetic variation} arises via (a) spontaneous point mutations in chromosomal genes (e.g., \textit{gyrA}, \textit{rpoB}), (b) horizontal gene transfer—conjugation (R-plasmids), transformation, and transduction—and (c) gene amplification/epigenetic regulation.
\item \textbf{Molecular resistance mechanisms} encoded by these genes include: enzymatic drug inactivation (β-lactamases, aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes), target-site modification (PBP2a in MRSA, \textit{erm} methyltransferase), active efflux pumps (AcrAB-TolC, MexAB-OprM), reduced membrane permeability (porin loss), alternative metabolic pathways, and biofilm/persister formation.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Prevention of spread in healthcare settings:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Antimicrobial stewardship}: restrict formulary, de-escalate within 48–72 h, limit duration, avoid unnecessary prophylaxis.
\item \textbf{Infection control}: rigorous hand hygiene, contact precautions, cohorting/isolation of colonised or infected patients, dedicated equipment, terminal environmental decontamination (sporicidal for \textit{C.\ difficile}, H\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{2} vapor for CRE).
\item \textbf{Active surveillance screening} at admission and ongoing (rectal/nasal swabs for MRSA, VRE, CRE).
\item \textbf{Rapid diagnostics} (PCR, MALDI-TOF, NGS) to enable targeted therapy within 24 h.
\item \textbf{Education and audit-and-feedback} for all clinical staff.
\item \textbf{Regulatory/policy actions}: limit OTC antibiotics, reduce agricultural use, mandate reporting of resistant isolates.
\item \textbf{Vaccination} to reduce overall bacterial-infection burden.
\item \textbf{Environmental engineering}: UV-C, aerosolised H\textsubscript{2}O\textsubscript{2}, water-system management.
\item \textbf{Sustained R\&D investment} in new agents, phage therapy, and adjuvants.
\end{enumerate}

