111953.y_answer:
\begin{minipage}{0.92\textwidth}
\textbf{Metal nanoparticles} possess a gapless, high-density-of-states electronic structure with delocalised conduction electrons. This gives rise to a \textbf{collective surface plasmon resonance} (a many-body oscillation of the electron gas) rather than individual band-to-band transitions. Plasmon decay is overwhelmingly non-radiative and, because of the very high DOS at the Fermi level, generated hot carriers thermalise in $<1$\,ps. Consequently, metal NPs are characterised by (i) intense, size/shape-tunable SPR absorption, (ii) efficient \textbf{photothermal conversion}, (iii) strong \textbf{local electromagnetic field enhancement} (SERS, catalysis), and (iv) short-lived hot-carrier redox chemistry, but weak photoluminescence and poor sustained photocatalysis.

\textbf{Semiconductor nanoparticles} possess a definite band gap $E_g$ with lower DOS and, below the Bohr radius, \textbf{quantum-confined, discrete} energy levels. Photoexcitation creates \textbf{Coulombically bound excitons} via single-particle transitions across $E_g$. The band gap acts as a recombination barrier and the lower DOS slows scattering, so excitons/electron–hole pairs survive for ns--$\mu$s. This yields (i) a sharp, \textbf{size-tunable} absorption edge and \textbf{tunable photoluminescence}, (ii) long-lived, separable charge carriers that drive \textbf{sustained multi-electron photocatalysis} (water splitting, pollutant degradation), and (iii) no intrinsic visible-range plasmonic resonance.

\textit{In essence, the presence or absence of a band gap, and the resulting difference between a delocalised, high-DOS electron gas (metals) and confined, gapped carriers (semiconductors), is the fundamental electronic-structure origin of every major photochemical distinction between the two classes of nanoparticles.}
\end{minipage}

